(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-l3-review-guided-quiz"]={"title":"ANS Quiz — Built to the Review Guidance","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient is given a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. Which set of findings should you expect?","choices":["Dry mouth, blurred vision, flushing and urinary retention","Salivation, tearing and a slow heart rate","Fasciculations and muscle weakness","Bronchospasm and diarrhoea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blocking muscarinic effects removes secretions, accommodation, and bladder tone &mdash; dry as a bone, blind as a bat, red as a beet. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A cholinergic agonist is given. Which effect on the eye should you expect?","choices":["Mydriasis","Miosis","Ptosis","Nystagmus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The M in the muscarinic effects list &mdash; pupillary constriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which finding indicates NICOTINIC rather than muscarinic stimulation?","choices":["Bronchorrhoea","Lacrimation","Fasciculations","Bradycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fasciculations sit in the nicotinic list alongside mydriasis, tachycardia, weakness and hypertension. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Nicotinic stimulation affects the heart rate and blood pressure in which direction?","choices":["Both fall","Rate falls, pressure rises","Rate rises, pressure falls","Both rise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tachycardia and hypertension are both on the nicotinic list. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A drug stimulates alpha-1 receptors. What should you expect to happen to the blood vessels and the blood pressure?","choices":["Vasoconstriction, and the pressure rises","Vasodilation, and the pressure falls","No vascular change, but the heart rate rises","Vasoconstriction, but the pressure falls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alpha-1 receptors sit on the effector organ; stimulating them constricts vessels and raises both systolic and diastolic pressure. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Clonidine stimulates alpha-2 receptors centrally. What should you expect as a result?","choices":["Increased sympathetic outflow and vasoconstriction","Reduced sympathetic outflow and vasodilation","Bronchodilation with no vascular effect","A direct increase in cardiac contractility"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It inhibits the sympathetic vasomotor centres, so less sympathetic traffic reaches the periphery. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A beta-1 agonist is given to a patient. Which two cardiac effects should you expect?","choices":["Decreased contractility and decreased rate","Increased contractility with a slower rate","Increased contractility and increased rate","No cardiac effect, but bronchodilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Positive inotrope and positive chronotrope &mdash; the two beta-1 cardiac actions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which effect follows stimulation of beta-2 receptors in the airway?","choices":["Bronchoconstriction","Increased bronchial secretions","No airway effect","Bronchodilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The basis of beta-2 agonist use in obstructive airway disease. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which structural description fits the nicotinic receptor?","choices":["A ligand-gated ion channel that admits sodium","A G protein-coupled receptor with five subtypes","An enzyme that hydrolyses acetylcholine","A nuclear receptor altering gene transcription"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Binding of two acetylcholine molecules changes its conformation and sodium flows in. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Dobutamine is a synthetic beta-1 agonist. Which use follows from that mechanism?","choices":["Relieving acute bronchospasm","Increasing cardiac output in acute heart failure","Lowering blood pressure in hypertension","Reversing a non-depolarizing blockade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A positive inotrope, and notably one that does not raise myocardial oxygen demand the way other sympathomimetics do. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Clonidine reduces sympathetic outflow. Besides hypertension, which use follows?","choices":["Treating acute asthma","Reversing anticholinergic poisoning","Easing withdrawal from opiates, tobacco and benzodiazepines","Inducing anaesthesia for intubation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Much of withdrawal is sympathetic overactivity, so damping the outflow blunts it. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Scopolamine acts more on the central nervous system than atropine does at usual doses. Which use follows from that?","choices":["Reversing neuromuscular blockade after surgery","Treating asthma","Managing pheochromocytoma","Preventing motion sickness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Its central action is the point; it also blocks short-term memory and sedates at lower doses. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Phentolamine competitively blocks alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors. Which use follows?","choices":["Short-term management of pheochromocytoma","Long-term treatment of asthma","Reversing succinylcholine","Raising blood pressure in shock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The tumour floods the circulation with catecholamines; blocking alpha receptors opposes the vasoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Succinylcholine has a rapid onset and short duration. Which use follows from that profile?","choices":["Long operations needing hours of paralysis","Rapid sequence intubation","Chronic treatment of muscle spasticity","Reversal of a competitive blockade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Also used for endotracheal intubation at induction, for the same reason. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Albuterol is a synthetic beta-2 agonist. Which conditions does that mechanism serve?","choices":["Hypertension","Acute heart failure","Asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Motion sickness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bronchodilation is the beta-2 airway action. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which of these would be most likely to SLOW a rapid heart rate?","choices":["An alpha-1 agonist","A beta-1 agonist","An antimuscarinic","An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Raising acetylcholine increases muscarinic activity at the heart, and bradycardia follows. A beta blocker or a muscarinic agonist would also work &mdash; there is more than one route. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A patient is symptomatically bradycardic. Which of these would RAISE the heart rate?","choices":["Atropine","Propranolol","Clonidine","Bethanechol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blocking muscarinic receptors at the heart removes the vagal brake. Beta-1 activation is the other route. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which of these would RAISE blood pressure?","choices":["Prazosin","Phenylephrine","Clonidine","Phentolamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An alpha-1 agonist constricts vessels and raises both systolic and diastolic pressure. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which of these is the only depolarizing neuromuscular blocker available?","choices":["Rocuronium","Vecuronium","Succinylcholine","Tubocurarine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every other neuromuscular blocker in use is non-depolarizing and competitive. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which of these is a selective beta-1 antagonist?","choices":["Propranolol","Timolol","Phenoxybenzamine","Metoprolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It sits with acebutolol, atenolol, bisoprolol and betaxolol in the selective group. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"Which of these is a selective alpha-1 blocker?","choices":["Tamsulosin","Phentolamine","Phenoxybenzamine","Clonidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It sits with prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin and alfuzosin. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which of these is an alpha-2 AGONIST?","choices":["Prazosin","Clonidine","Phenylephrine","Phenoxybenzamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It acts centrally at alpha-2 receptors to reduce sympathetic outflow. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which of these blocks alpha receptors IRREVERSIBLY, so that new receptors must be synthesised?","choices":["Phentolamine","Prazosin","Phenoxybenzamine","Propranolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-selective and non-competitive, which is what makes the blockade irreversible. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"Which group do epinephrine, norepinephrine, isoproterenol and dopamine belong to?","choices":["The indirect-acting agonists","The mixed-action agonists","The ganglionic blockers","The catecholamines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They share high potency and rapid inactivation by catechol-O-methyltransferase and monoamine oxidase. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which description belongs to the anticholinergic toxidrome?","choices":["Hot as Hades &mdash; hyperthermia","Cold and clammy from sweating","Pinpoint pupils","Copious salivation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It sits with mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet and dry as a bone. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which central feature marks the severe end of anticholinergic toxicity?","choices":["Flaccid paralysis","Seizures","Bradycardia","Bronchorrhoea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The mad-as-a-hatter progression runs from sedation and anxiety through hallucinations to seizures. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Pralidoxime is given for acetylcholinesterase inhibitor poisoning. What is its key limitation?","choices":["It only works on muscarinic and not nicotinic signs","It must be given before the exposure","It does not penetrate the central nervous system","It cannot be given with atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It reactivates inhibited enzyme peripherally, and it also cannot overcome reversible inhibitors such as physostigmine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Physostigmine, neostigmine and pyridostigmine all inhibit acetylcholinesterase. What separates physostigmine from the other two?","choices":["It is the only one that is reversible","It is the only one given by mouth","It works at nicotinic receptors only","It crosses the blood-brain barrier"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is why it is the one used when anticholinergic toxicity is central; the others act peripherally. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"During which phase of succinylcholine's action does flaccid paralysis appear?","choices":["Phase two","Phase one","Before any depolarization occurs","Only after the drug is metabolised"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Phase one is the depolarization that produces fasciculations; the receptor then desensitises, and phase two is the paralysis. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which adverse effects should a patient starting albuterol be warned about?","choices":["Dry mouth and urinary retention","Tremor, restlessness and anxiety","Bradycardia and bronchospasm","Sedation and memory impairment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The expected beta-agonist effects &mdash; unpleasant rather than dangerous. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 98"}]};
